<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otjimbingwe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otjimbingwe is a settlement on the Swakop River in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windhoek">Windhoek</a>. A Rhenish mission station was founded there in 1849, and it became a trading and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-mining centre on the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walvis-bay">Walvis Bay</a>. In 1863 the Herero under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharero">Maharero</a> fought there to win independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oorlam">Oorlam</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaners</a>, and in the late 1880s it served as the administrative seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otjimbingwe.jpg" alt="Otjimbingwe" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arche-foto; Burkhart Rüchel · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rheinische_missionskirche_otjimbingwe_namibia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
